Trace of what is actually in this file:

  1. _lpTokenValue() reads balances(0)/balances(1), builds the token/decimals arrays, and computes ONE spot price —
  spotPrices[SECONDARY_INDEX] — from get_dy(PRIMARY, SECONDARY, dyAmount), normalized to 18 decimals. The primary-index
  entry is deliberately left at zero (the comment says so).
  2. It then hands tokens, decimals, balances, spotPrices to _calculateLPTokenValue(...) and converts to int256.
  3. _calculateBaseToQuote() multiplies that by a Chainlink baseToUSD feed, with the only require in this file being
  baseToUSD > 0.

  The entire claimed exploit lives inside _calculateLPTokenValue, which is in AbstractLPOracle and is not shown. The
  finding asserts a specific control-flow fact about code that is not in the input: that there is a require comparing
  the get_dy-derived spot price against an oracle price with a 0.99e18 lower limit that reverts. Nothing in the provided
  source contains 0.99e18, any limit constant, or any deviation require. _lowerLimitMultiplier/_upperLimitMultiplier
  are constructor arguments forwarded to the abstract base; their values, the units they are applied in, which of the
  two spot-price slots the check actually consumes (recall one of the two is intentionally zero, so the base must be
  skipping or special-casing zero entries), and whether the comparison reverts or clamps are all unverifiable from what
  I was given. I cannot point to the exact line that breaks, nor confirm the sign/direction of the comparison, nor the
  magnitude of swap needed to trip it — the "~13% of TVL" and "below 0.99e18" numbers are asserted, not derived from any
  shown formula.

  Second, even taking the deviation check as existing, its semantics matter and cut the other way. A spot-vs-oracle
  divergence check that reverts is a fail-closed manipulation guard: its designed purpose is to refuse to price the LP
  when the pool's instantaneous state disagrees with the trusted feed. Reverting under exactly the condition the
  attacker creates is the guard doing its job, not a bypass of it. Turning that into a Medium finding requires showing
  the base contract's threshold is set so tight that ordinary, non-manipulated pool states or economically cheap
  manipulation trip it — which requires reading the base contract and the deployment parameters, neither of which is
  available here. dyAmount is explicitly documented as customizable per-pool to account for pool size, which is a
  deliberate knob for tuning exactly this sensitivity; the finding does not engage with it beyond assuming a bad value.

  Third, the round-trip cost claim ("pure round-trip swap fees") is asserted without accounting for the fact that a
  stableswap swap large enough to move the fee-inclusive get_dy by >1% leaves the attacker paying both legs of fees plus
  the imbalance penalty, repeated every block, against an unbounded liquidation window — and the same revert also
  freezes borrows/mints priced by the same LP, which is the fail-closed intent.

  By the stated tie-break, the decisive code — the deviation require, its limits, and its handling of the zero-valued
  primary spot slot — is not in the material I was asked to audit, so I am not confident the exploit holds as described.

  CONSTRAINT|C2|A denial-of-service claim resting on a sanity-check revert must quote the actual comparison, threshold,
  and units from the code that implements the check rather than asserting them, and must show the revert condition is
  reachable by ordinary or cheaply-forced state rather than being the fail-closed guard operating as designed.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/oracles/Curve2TokenOracle.sol:_lpTokenValue:88|C2|The claimed deviation require and its 0.99e18
  limit live in _calculateLPTokenValue inside the un-shown AbstractLPOracle, so the decisive control-flow fact is
  unverifiable here, and to the extent such a check exists its revert-on-divergence is the intended fail-closed
  manipulation guard rather than a bypass of one.
